This repository documents my hands-on experience and security testing workflow conducted on February 15th, using Ubuntu Linux. The focus was on network scanning, brute-forcing, and web vulnerability analysis.
 Tools & Technologies Used
OS: Ubuntu Linux
Network/Web Tools: Nmap, Wireshark, Dirsearch, Gobuster
Exploitation Tools: Hydra, sqlmap
Proxy/Interception: Burp Suite Community Edition
 Activities Performed
1. Service Brute-Forcing with Hydra
Target: ://testphp.vulnweb.com
Goal: Identifying valid credentials for a web login form.
Execution:
Analyzed HTTP POST parameters (uname, pass).
Configured Hydra with a custom wordlist and defined success criteria (S=Logout) to eliminate False Positives.
Result: Successfully identified valid credentials: test:test.
2. SQL Injection Analysis with sqlmap
Focus: Automated vulnerability detection in database-driven web applications.
Tasks:
Tested various injection points (URL parameters, User-Agent, and Referer headers).
Identified Boolean-based blind and Union-based SQL injection vulnerabilities on the cat parameter.
Explored the database structure (acuart database) and practiced data extraction commands for the users table.
3. Traffic Interception with Burp Suite
Workflow:
Set up a local proxy to intercept real-time HTTP/HTTPS traffic.
Used the Intercept feature to capture and modify login requests before they reached the server.
Explored the Intruder module to set payload positions and automate password spraying attacks.
Used Repeater for manual request manipulation and testing SQL Injection payloads like admin' OR '1'='1.
4. Directory Brute-Forcing
Practiced using Gobuster to discover hidden directories and files on the target web server using common wordlists.
 Key Learnings
Understanding the importance of Success/Failure string matching in automated tools to prevent False Positives.
Differentiating between Blind SQL Injection (time-based/boolean-based) and Union-based methods.
Learning the manual request/response lifecycle by intercepting packets with Burp Suite.
Disclaimer: All tests were conducted on vulnweb.com, a legal and safe environment specifically designed for security testing and education.
